{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a868cada85101508","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tepic_count: 8\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9013: FIPS 140-3 compliance for the model serving stack\n11\t\n12\t## Epic List\n13\t\n14\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n15\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n16\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 | Platform Operator midstream repository onboarding | Implementation | RHOAI Platform | P0 |\n17\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 | Platform Operator transitive dependency license validation | Implementation | RHOAI Platform | P0 |\n18\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 | Platform Operator Konflux build pipeline onboarding | Implementation | RHOAI Platform | P0 |\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 | Platform Operator FIPS crypto remediation | Implementation | RHOAI Platform | P0 |\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E005 | KServe serving-stack image FIPS crypto remediation | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E006 | FIPS regression gating CI check for serving-stack builds | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P1 |\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E007 | FIPS compliance evidence package and certification submission | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E008 | FIPS support posture documentation | Implementation | RHOAI Docs | P0 |\n24\t\n25\t## Dependency DAG\n26\t\n27\t```mermaid\n28\tgraph TD\n29\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 repo onboarding] --> E003[RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 Konflux onboarding]\n30\t    E001 --> E004[RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 Platform Operator FIPS remediation]\n31\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 license validation] --> E004\n32\t    E004 --> E007[RHAISTRAT-9013-E007 evidence package]\n33\t    E005[RHAISTRAT-9013-E005 KServe FIPS remediation] --> E007\n34\t    E006[RHAISTRAT-9013-E006 CI gating check]\n35\t    E001 --> E008[RHAISTRAT-9013-E008 docs]\n36\t    E002 --> E008\n37\t    E003 --> E008\n38\t    E004 --> E008\n39\t    E005 --> E008\n40\t    E006 --> E008\n41\t    E007 --> E008\n42\t```\n43\t\n44\tDeliberately absent edges (mandated parallelism):\n45\t\n46\t- E001 ↔ E002 (Rule 7) and E002 ↔ E003 (Rule 8): license validation runs parallel to both onboarding epics.\n47\t- E003 ↔ E004 (Rule 10): the Konflux pipeline and the FIPS remediation run in parallel; the coupling is\n48\t  expressed as the \"build pipeline green\" acceptance criterion on E004, never as a DAG edge.\n49\t- E003 → E007: build attestations from the Platform Operator pipeline are consumed as *content* by the\n50\t  evidence package — expressed as an acceptance criterion on E007 (Rule 13), not an edge.\n51\t- E005 ↔ E006 and E006 ↔ E007: the CI gating check is independent work (pipeline configuration vs. image\n52\t  rebuilds and evidence assembly) with no shared build-against artifact (Rules 14/15).\n53\t\n54\t## DAG Justification\n55\t\n56\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n57\t|------|------|-----------|\n58\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 5 | `repo-onboarding` → `konflux-onboarding` always serial; the pipeline needs the repo. |\n59\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 6 | General implementation of the onboarded component needs the repository to exist; does not block the KServe work. |\n60\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 9 | `license-validation` → general implementation serial: if licenses fail, the dependency set changes and the remediation approach changes. |\n61\t| E004 → E007 | Rule 13 | The evidence package's content (module inventory, FIPS-mode test results) is the deliverable of the remediation; it documents what was built, so it is serial after it (same rationale direction as Rule 11). |\n62\t| E005 → E007 | Rule 13 | Same: evidence of the remediated KServe images cannot be assembled before the remediation lands. |\n63\t| all Impls → E008 | Rule 11 | `docs-authoring` is blocked by every Implementation epic; docs describe what was built. These edges do not push priority (docs-authoring priority exception). |\n64\t\n65\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n66\t\n67\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n68\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n69\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E004, E005 | Image remediation split by component (KServe / Platform Operator) per epic boundary rule 1. E001–E003 are enabling epics for the Platform Operator half (no direct HLR; P0 by priority inheritance — they enable P0 remediation work). |\n70\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E007 | Evidence package (module inventory, build attestations, test results) assembled and submitted through the security review board to the external certification lab. |\n71\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E006 | CI gating check. Kept as its own epic: it is a distinct, deferrable feature (regression prevention) that shares no data structure, UI surface, or API endpoint with the image remediation — it configures the build pipelines, not the images. |\n72\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E008 | Substantive new documentation (FIPS support posture, cluster prerequisites) — a distinct work stream, so a separate `docs-authoring` epic rather than scope inside the remediation epics. Its priority is P0 via the docs-authoring exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical` → P0), not from HLR-4's P2 label; with that derivation the P2 label reads as relative value, not a deferral signal. |\n73\t\n74\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n75\t\n76\t## Health Warnings\n77\t\n78\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no\n79\t  Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no non-functional requirements. Decomposition proceeded from the\n80\t  HLR table, affected components, and problem statement. Owner teams were inferred: RHOAI Serving is taken\n81\t  from the KServe architecture-context file; **RHOAI Platform** (Platform Operator) and **RHOAI Docs**\n82\t  (documentation) are assumptions — verify with the strategy author before scheduling.\n83\t- **Platform Operator is absent from architecture context**: only\n84\t  `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md` exists. Per the platform-membership rule\n85\t  (a component is \"in the platform\" only if it has an architecture-context file), Platform Operator was\n86\t  treated as a new component and given the full onboarding chain (E001–E003). If the Platform Operator\n87\t  already exists in the platform inventory and the context file is merely missing, E001–E003 should be\n88\t  re-scoped or dropped and E004 re-based onto the existing repos/pipelines — human verification recommended.\n89\t- **Externally controlled schedule (not an AC anywhere)**: the accredited-lab turnaround (historically six\n90\t  to nine months) and the security review board cadence are outside engineering control. They are recorded\n91\t  as schedule risks on E007, and E007's acceptance criteria stop at the engineering completion point we do\n92\t  control (evidence package assembled and submitted/accepted for triage). No epic carries \"lab completes\n93\t  certification\" or \"review board signs off within N weeks\" as an acceptance criterion.\n94\t- **Effort / re-split triggers**: E004 and E005 are open-ended audits-plus-remediation over \"several\n95\t  images\". Each is kept as one logical change (a single audit + mechanical remediation pass over one\n96\t  component's image set; splitting would duplicate the audit). Re-split trigger: if the crypto-usage audit\n97\t  surfaces more than a handful of distinct images or more than one crypto-library family per component,\n98\t  split the remediation per image family. E007 similarly: split the logistics/submission tracking out if\n99\t  the evidence assembly outgrows a sprint of effort.\n100\t- **Priority note**: HLR-4 is labelled P2 in the strategy while the docs epic is P0 by the docs-authoring\n101\t  priority derivation (strategy `priority: Critical`). This is a derivation artifact, not a priority\n102\t  inversion in the plan.\n103\t\n104\t## Tiered Delivery\n105\t\n106\tNot applied as an epic split. The external dependency with uncertain timing (the certification lab) does\n107\tnot gate any engineering epic: HLR-2's engineering completion point is assembly and *submission* of the\n108\tevidence package, which is fully within our control. All engineering work (remediation, CI enforcement,\n109\tdocs, evidence assembly) delivers independent value without the lab; the certification *outcome* is a\n110\tpost-submission external process recorded as a schedule risk on E007, so no Tier 2 (dependency-gated) epic\n111\tset exists.\n112\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-988037398ae6e219","agent_id":"a7e929e672b4dfe9b","session_id":"f37d0be2-e15d-4b1a-8282-2b0c8101e79b","uuid":"11afe217-5a26-4485-aa7a-51bde28f987c","timestamp":"2026-10-23T18:52:19.240Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a868cada85101508","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9013 decomposition"}
