check: all 11 diagram edges have matching `dependencies`\nentries and vice versa; no edge references a nonexistent epic; arrows run dependency →\ndependent per the `graph TD` convention; no cycles. No branch files exist and no\nfrontmatter sets `branch`/`gated_by`, consistently.\n\nEvery edge is justified by a construction rule: E001→E003 (Rule 5), E001→E004 (Rule 6),\nE002→E004 (Rule 9), E004/E005→E007 (genuine data dependencies — the module inventories\nand FIPS-mode test results the evidence package documents are deliverables of the\nremediation epics, not build-pipeline outputs, so serial edges are the correct shape),\nall Impl→E008 (Rule 11, all seven Implementation epics, no priority push).\n\nImplementation-type parallelism enumerated across every pair: no edge between E002 and\nE001/E003 (Rules 7/8) and no edge between E003 and any of the four general\nimplementation epics E004/E005/E006/E007 (Rule 10 — including E007, the evidence epic\nconsuming the pipeline's outputs). The E003→E007 attestations coupling is correctly an\nacceptance criterion on E007 (\"build attestations for every remediated serving-stack\nimage\"), not a DAG edge; its absence from the DAG is the rule-compliant shape and is not\ncounted as a missing dependency. E006 runs parallel to E005 (Rule 15, same repo\ndifferent areas) and to E007 (Rule 14), and its coupling to the Platform Operator\npipeline (E003) is correctly an AC (\"runs on both KServe and Platform Operator\nserving-stack builds\"), never an edge. Critical path E001→E004→E007→E008, length 4 —\nwithin the M-with-new-component heuristic (4-5).\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n\nComponent/team tuples are clean: E001-E004 (Platform Operator / RHOAI Platform),\nE005-E007 (KServe / RHOAI Serving), E008 (Documentation / RHOAI Docs). The E004/E005\nremediation split is by component per boundary rule 1, not a duplication — each audits\nand remediates a different image set; the identically-named deliverable (module\ninventory) is the same logical change applied to different repos, not two epics building\none surface. No epic mixes code implementation with content authoring; the docs work is\na separate `docs-authoring` epic.\n\nOversized-epic handling: E004, E005, and E007 are open-ended audit-plus-remediation\nepics, but each is one logical change and the summary's Health Warnings record concrete\nre-split triggers (per image family if the audit surfaces more than a handful of images\nor more than one crypto-library family; split logistics out of E007 if assembly outgrows\na sprint). Per the review rule these are managed watch items, not defects, and no\nfurther split is suggested.\n\nOne minor: E006 and E007 carry `component: KServe` while their scope explicitly spans\nPlatform Operator pipelines/evidence as well. The single-epic shape is right (splitting\nthe CI check or the evidence package per component would duplicate the work), and the\nbodies state the cross-component scope clearly, but the component routing label\nunder-describes it — see issues list.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n\nAll eight epics are Implementation and each produces a concrete artifact (repo,\nlicense report, pipeline, remediated images, CI check, evidence package, docs). No epic\nis misclassified as Investigation: the crypto-usage audits inside E004/E005 are\nimplementation work resolving enumerated defects, not scope-changing unknowns, and the\nstrategy explicitly states there are no open questions on scope. `gated_by` is null\neverywhere with no Investigation dependencies, so the gating metadata rules are\nsatisfied vacuously.\n\nThe Platform Operator platform-membership question (no architecture-context file, so\ntreated as a new component and given the Rule 20 onboarding chain) was checked: the\npipeline's membership rule is deterministic (a component is in the platform iff it has\na context file), so E001-E003 follow the rule, and the \"context file merely missing\"\npossibility is recorded in Health Warnings with a concrete re-scope path rather than\nsilently assumed — defensible, not a type error.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n\nAll eight epics are Implementation and carry `ai_signals` (not `investigation_signals`);\nall eight `-ai-signals.md` rationale files exist and their values match the frontmatter.\n\n`architecture_claims` calibration checked against `.context`: the four Platform\nOperator epics (E001-E004) score 0 with the correct no-context-file rationale and a\npointer to the Health Warning; the four epics touching KServe (E005-E008) score +1 and\ncite `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md` (GA status,\nmidstream repos, Konflux onboarding, deployment modes) — the strategy's premise about\nthe serving stack is exactly what that file corroborates. E007's `external_dependency:\n-1` (accredited lab) and `human_process_gates: -1` (review board sign-off) match the\nstrategy's stated gates; E008's `human_process_gates: -1` (docs editorial/technical\nreview) matches its Rule 24 review AC. Computed totals were not checked per\ninstructions.\n\nOne minor: `pattern_precedent` is scored 0 on E004/E005/E007 with rationales scoped to\n\"no precedent visible in the platform context\". That is an honest-unknown 0 rather than\na rubric contradiction, but for E005 the target repo (opendatahub-io/kserve, scored\n`repo_access: 1`) is accessible, so precedent there is checkable — see issues list.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n\nEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy. Rule-mandated ACs verified on\nevery applicable epic: E008 (docs-authoring) has the technical-review AC; E004 and E005\nhave build-pipeline-green ACs (Rule 25; E004's covers the Rule 10 coupling to E003, and\nE007's attestations AC covers the same coupling from the consuming side); no epic\nreplaces an existing user-facing capability, so Rule 23 rollback ACs do not apply. E006\nincludes a negative test proving the check fails a reintroducing build —