{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9d12bb8e047878cb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n9\t  description: 'E006 and E007 carry component: KServe while their scope spans Platform\n10\t    Operator pipelines/evidence too; the single-epic shape is correct (a per-component\n11\t    split would duplicate the CI check / evidence package), but the component routing\n12\t    label under-describes the stack-wide scope — note the cross-component scope in\n13\t    the summary or pick a routing convention for stack-wide epics'\n14\t- severity: minor\n15\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n16\t  description: 'pattern_precedent scored 0 on E004/E005/E007 with rationales scoped\n17\t    to no precedent visible in the platform context; for E005 the target repo (opendatahub-io/kserve,\n18\t    repo_access: 1) is accessible so codebase precedent is checkable — verify and\n19\t    cite it, or score -1 where the -1 condition (no precedent in codebase) is confirmed'\n20\t- severity: minor\n21\t  criterion: Acceptance Criteria Quality\n22\t  description: E006 consumes the validated-modules list established by the E004/E005\n23\t    remediation as its detection criterion, but the coupling is only in scope prose\n24\t    — add an AC on E006 gating the detection criterion on the remediation-established\n25\t    validated-modules list (config reference, correctly not a DAG edge)\n26\terror: null\n27\t---\n28\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9013\n29\t\n30\t## Review Summary\n31\t\n32\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n33\tRecommendation: accept\n34\t\n35\tContract validator: OK (`validate_decomposition.py` exits 0 — body sections, canonical\n36\tcomponents, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, and Rules 7/8/10\n37\tparallelism all pass). Triage consistency: `triage: null` (full decomposition), so the\n38\t8-epic multi-epic output is expected and not penalized.\n39\t\n40\t## Criterion Details\n41\t\n42\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n43\t\n44\tAll four HLRs map to epics: HLR-1 (P0) → E004 + E005 split by component per boundary\n45\trule 1; HLR-2 (P0) → E007; HLR-3 (P1) → E006; HLR-4 (P2) → E008. Traceability matrix\n46\tpresent in the summary, and every epic's HLR Traceability section agrees with it.\n47\t\n48\tPriority inheritance verified edge by edge: E001 and E002 block the P0 remediation and\n49\tare P0; E003, E004, E005, E007 block or feed P0 work and are P0; E006 (P1) blocks only\n50\tE008, a `docs-authoring` epic exempt from priority inheritance — no violation. E008's\n51\tP0 is correctly derived from the strategy's `priority: Critical` via the docs-authoring\n52\texception, and the HLR-4 matrix row records that derivation so the P2 label reads as\n53\trelative value, not a deferral signal.\n54\t\n55\tNo priority collapse: each HLR sits in its own epic. The HLR-3 matrix row records the\n56\tStep-4 split analysis (distinct deferrable feature, no shared data structure or API\n57\tsurface with the image remediation), and the HLR-4 row records the docs-authoring\n58\tderivation — both analyses are in the matrix rows themselves, where the mapping is read.\n59\t\n60\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n61\t\n62\tFrontmatter-to-diagram cross-check: all 11 diagram edges have matching `dependencies`\n63\tentries and vice versa; no edge references a nonexistent epic; arrows run dependency →\n64\tdependent per the `graph TD` convention; no cycles. No branch files exist and no\n65\tfrontmatter sets `branch`/`gated_by`, consistently.\n66\t\n67\tEvery edge is justified by a construction rule: E001→E003 (Rule 5), E001→E004 (Rule 6),\n68\tE002→E004 (Rule 9), E004/E005→E007 (genuine data dependencies — the module inventories\n69\tand FIPS-mode test results the evidence package documents are deliverables of the\n70\tremediation epics, not build-pipeline outputs, so serial edges are the correct shape),\n71\tall Impl→E008 (Rule 11, all seven Implementation epics, no priority push).\n72\t\n73\tImplementation-type parallelism enumerated across every pair: no edge between E002 and\n74\tE001/E003 (Rules 7/8) and no edge between E003 and any of the four general\n75\timplementation epics E004/E005/E006/E007 (Rule 10 — including E007, the evidence epic\n76\tconsuming the pipeline's outputs). The E003→E007 attestations coupling is correctly an\n77\tacceptance criterion on E007 (\"build attestations for every remediated serving-stack\n78\timage\"), not a DAG edge; its absence from the DAG is the rule-compliant shape and is not\n79\tcounted as a missing dependency. E006 runs parallel to E005 (Rule 15, same repo\n80\tdifferent areas) and to E007 (Rule 14), and its coupling to the Platform Operator\n81\tpipeline (E003) is correctly an AC (\"runs on both KServe and Platform Operator\n82\tserving-stack builds\"), never an edge. Critical path E001→E004→E007→E008, length 4 —\n83\twithin the M-with-new-component heuristic (4-5).\n84\t\n85\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n86\t\n87\tComponent/team tuples are clean: E001-E004 (Platform Operator / RHOAI Platform),\n88\tE005-E007 (KServe / RHOAI Serving), E008 (Documentation / RHOAI Docs). The E004/E005\n89\tremediation split is by component per boundary rule 1, not a duplication — each audits\n90\tand remediates a different image set; the identically-named deliverable (module\n91\tinventory) is the same logical change applied to different repos, not two epics building\n92\tone surface. No epic mixes code implementation with content authoring; the docs work is\n93\ta separate `docs-authoring` epic.\n94\t\n95\tOversized-epic handling: E004, E005, and E007 are open-ended audit-plus-remediation\n96\tepics, but each is one logical change and the summary's Health Warnings record concrete\n97\tre-split triggers (per image family if the audit surfaces more than a handful of images\n98\tor more than one crypto