from architecture context**: only\n83\t  `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md` exists. Per the platform-membership rule\n84\t  (a component is \"in the platform\" only if it has an architecture-context file), Platform Operator was\n85\t  treated as a new component and given the full onboarding chain (E001–E003). If the Platform Operator\n86\t  already exists in the platform inventory and the context file is merely missing, E001–E003 should be\n87\t  re-scoped or dropped and E004 re-based onto the existing repos/pipelines — human verification recommended.\n88\t- **Externally controlled schedule (not an AC anywhere)**: the accredited-lab turnaround (historically six\n89\t  to nine months) and the security review board cadence are outside engineering control. They are recorded\n90\t  as schedule risks on E007, and E007's acceptance criteria stop at the engineering completion point we do\n91\t  control (evidence package assembled and submitted/accepted for triage). No epic carries \"lab completes\n92\t  certification\" or \"review board signs off within N weeks\" as an acceptance criterion.\n93\t- **Effort / re-split triggers**: E004 and E005 are open-ended audits-plus-remediation over \"several\n94\t  images\". Each is kept as one logical change (a single audit + mechanical remediation pass over one\n95\t  component's image set; splitting would duplicate the audit). Re-split trigger: if the crypto-usage audit\n96\t  surfaces more than a handful of distinct images or more than one crypto-library family per component,\n97\t  split the remediation per image family. E007 similarly: split the logistics/submission tracking out if\n98\t  the evidence assembly outgrows a sprint of effort.\n99\t- **Priority note**: HLR-4 is labelled P2 in the strategy while the docs epic is P0 by the docs-authoring\n100\t  priority derivation (strategy `priority: Critical`). This is a derivation artifact, not a priority\n101\t  inversion in the plan.\n102\t\n103\t## T