nt) against frontmatter is an exact match: E003:[E001],\nE004:[E001,E002], E007:[E004,E005], E008:[E001–E007]; E001/E002/E005/E006 have no dependencies —\n12 edges in both directions, no mismatches. Implementation-type parallelism verified for every\npair: no E001↔E002 edge (Rule 7), no E002↔E003 edge (Rule 8), and critically no E003→E004 /\nE003→E005 / E003→E006 / E003→E007 edge (Rule 10) — the Konflux onboarding runs parallel to every\ngeneral implementation epic. The E003↔E004 coupling is correctly expressed as the Rule 25\n\"build pipeline green\" AC on E004, and the E003→E007 attestations link is correctly an AC gate\non E007 (build attestations for every remediated serving-stack image), not a DAG edge — the\nbuild-pipeline-output-as-content shape is rule-compliant and its absence from the DAG is not a\nmissing dependency. E002→E004 (Rule 9, license-validation → general implementation) and\nE004/E005→E007 (evidence cannot precede the remediation it documents) are justified serial\nedges. All-implementation→E008 edges are Rule 11 docs-last edges and do not trigger priority\ninheritance. Critical path length 4 (E001→E004→E007→E008) is within the M-with-new-component\nheuristic (4–5). No conditional branch files exist and no `gated_by` is set anywhere, so no\nbranch/gate consistency checks apply.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\nComponent/team tuples are clean: E001–E004 (Platform Operator / RHOAI Platform), E005–E007\n(KServe / RHOAI Serving), E008 (Documentation / RHOAI Docs). E006 and E007 carry `component:\nKServe` while their scope spans both KServe and Platform Operator builds — this is the\nsame-logical-change exemption correctly applied (one check, one detection criterion, one\nevidence package per HLR-2's stack-wide framing; a per-component split would duplicate one\ndeliverable), and the routing convention is documented in the summary with the scope stated in\neach epic's body. No sibling scope duplication: E004 and E005 audit and remediate disjoint\ncomponent image sets; E003's \"pipeline green on main\" and E004's \"pipeline green for the\nremediated images\" are distinct gates (initial onboarding vs. first remediated build). No\nwork-product type mixing — docs authoring is isolated in E008. Size: E004/E005 (audit +\nremediation over \"several images\") and E007 are potential overages, but each is one logical\nchange and the summary's Health Warnings record concrete re-split triggers (per image family\nfor the remediations; logistics split for E007) — managed watch items, not defects.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\nNo Investigation epics exist, and that is correct: the strategy states \"None on scope\" for open\nquestions, and the residual uncertainties (which images/libraries; whether Platform Operator is\ntruly absent from the platform inventory) do not change which downstream epics exist — the\naudits are embedded as the first scope item of E004/E005, and the Platform Operator\nplatform-membership question is recorded as a Health Warning with a concrete fallback\n(E001–E003 re-scope/drop) rather than modeled as a gate. Every Implementation epic produces a\nconcrete artifact (repository, license report, green pipeline with attestations, remediated\nimages, gating CI check, evidence package, published docs). No `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact`\nfields are set anywhere, so there are no gating-metadata inconsistencies and no Investigation\ndependencies missing `gated_by`.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\nAll 8 epics are Implementation type and all carry `ai_signals` (the 9-signal rubric) with\n`investigation_signals: null` — type-appropriate. All 8 ai-signals rationale files exist and\ntheir values match the frontmatter. Signal values are consistent with the strategy content:\nE007's `external_dependency: -1` (accredited lab) and `human_process_gates: -1` (security\nreview board) match the problem statement; E005's `pattern_precedent: -1` is justified with a\nverified check against the actual repo surfaces; E008's `human_process_gates: -1` matches its\nmandatory technical-review AC. Architecture-claims calibration checked against `.context`: only\n`architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md` exists, and the scoring follows — E005/E006/E007/E008 score\n`architecture_claims: 1` citing the kserve context file, while E001–E004 score 0 with the\nhonest no-context-rule rationale for Platform Operator (flagged for human verification in the\nsummary) rather than a false \"no claims\" assertion. Computed scores (`ai_implementability`,\n`ai_implementability_score`) were not arithmetic-checked per the review contract.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\nEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy. Rule-mandated ACs are present where\napplicable: E003 carries the build-pipeline-green AC for the Konflux chain; E004 carries the\nRule 25 pipeline-green AC that couples it to E003 without a DAG edge; E008 carries the\ntechnical-review AC for docs-authoring; E006 includes a negative test proving the check fails a\nreintroducing build. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID (verified by grep — all\nE-number references are frontmatter `dependencies` entries). Two minor issues: (1) E007's final\nAC ends \"and accepted for triage\" — acceptance for triage is an administrative act granted by\nthe external lab, so the AC's completion point is partially outside engineering control even\nthough the decomposition otherwise deliberately stops at submission (the summary's Health\nWarning says exactly that); (2) E004/E005 replace crypto module usage without any rollback\nconsideration in ACs or notes — prior image tags presumably remain available, but the\nreplacement work should say so. Two minors in this criterion do not cost a point.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\nAll strategy scope is covered: both affected components have remediation paths (KServe\nimmediately via existing repos/pipelines — corroborated by the kserve architecture context;\nPlatform Operator via the onboarding chain), the serving data plane images are explicitly in\nE005's audit scope, both serving deployment modes (serverless and raw) are in E005's functional\nACs, HLR-2's evidence package covers both components' inventories and attestations, HLR-3's\ncheck covers both KServe and Platform Operator builds, and HLR-4's documentation (posture +\ncluster prerequisites + out-of-scope statement) is E008. Out-of-scope boundaries (workbenches,\npipelines, training, Common Criteria) are respected and restated in E008. Strategy-level\ncontext is carried forward, not dropped: the externally controlled lab turnaround and\nreview-board cadence are recorded as schedule risks on E007 and in the summary's Health\nWarnings rather than as ACs; the missing strategy sections (teams, dependencies, NFRs) are\nflagged with the inferred-owner assumptions called out for verification. Tiered delivery is\nreasoned: no engineering epic is gated on the certification outcome, so no Tier 2 set exists.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":37619,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8b0ffec040526743","agent_id":"a02b9e912b910f608","session_id":"f37d0be2-e15d-4b1a-8282-2b0c8101e79b","uuid":"1c01f694-d426-4113-8b53-77607b15e04d","timestamp":"2026-10-23T19:04:07.682Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9013 decomposition"}
